WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:05.269
The project starts with the kick-off: goal, scope, roles, first steps.

2
00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:09.854
An hour, and the sponsor says in his own words why it matters.

3
00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:15.843
Then, in parallel, quick wins, small visible fixes from the people themselves,

4
00:00:15.993 --> 00:00:18.907
and foundations: baseline, roles, rhythm.

5
00:00:19.923 --> 00:00:24.462
Quick wins alone are a show; foundations alone, and people drop out.

6
00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:27.864
Roughly one or two a week, for four weeks.

7
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:31.443
The first two weeks, be on the floor.

8
00:00:32.166 --> 00:00:36.531
Ask before you suggest; stand at a changeover, ride along with a crew.

9
00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:42.028
What you see there, like 40 metres walked for a tool, beats any report.

10
00:00:42.811 --> 00:00:44.483
Note times and counts.

11
00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:50.528
Trust: write down every promise, and come back within the week, even with a no.

12
00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:53.955
We tried this in 2019?

13
00:00:54.639 --> 00:00:55.452
Often true.

14
00:00:56.163 --> 00:00:59.019
Ask what went wrong then; that is what to avoid.

15
00:01:00.068 --> 00:01:03.017
Ask in week 4: do we know the starting number yet?

16
00:01:03.806 --> 00:01:05.292
Or the baseline waits.

17
00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:08.808
Week 1 at a pump assembly plant.

18
00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:15.107
Second shift lacks a wrench that tightens bolts exactly: about 20 minutes a shift.

19
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.821
Through the team lead, a second one arrives Wednesday, for 400 euros.

20
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.753
Meanwhile a two-week stop log as the baseline,

21
00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.468
and every Tuesday at nine a check with the production manager.

22
00:01:29.482 --> 00:01:33.720
By week 3 operators bring problems unasked: the trust is there.

23
00:01:35.004 --> 00:01:38.325
Set the rhythm early: fixed day, time and agenda.

24
00:01:39.042 --> 00:01:43.002
Progress against the goal, what is blocked, which decisions are needed.

25
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:44.926
Three meetings.

26
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:50.457
The stand-up: fifteen minutes standing at the board, two or three times a week.

27
00:01:51.171 --> 00:01:52.982
Done, next, what blocks you?

28
00:01:53.976 --> 00:01:59.003
The weekly: 45 to 60 minutes with the team, or 30 with the process owner.

29
00:02:00.056 --> 00:02:05.094
The steering group, every four to six weeks, 60 to 90 minutes, where the sponsor,

30
00:02:05.269 --> 00:02:09.135
who owns goal and budget, and a few department heads decide.

31
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.312
So you see problems in week three, not month four.

32
00:02:14.624 --> 00:02:19.698
A beverage plant wants less downtime: hours a line stands still when it should run.

33
00:02:20.448 --> 00:02:25.267
Stand-up Monday and Thursday at half past eight, at the board: fifteen minutes,

34
00:02:25.371 --> 00:02:26.137
five people.

35
00:02:27.192 --> 00:02:34.042
Weekly on Tuesday at one, 45 minutes: 10 actions, 15 progress, 15 decisions,

36
00:02:34.193 --> 00:02:35.307
5 looking ahead.

37
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.911
The steering group: every five weeks, an hour, three decision items.

38
00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:47.687
An agenda ends in decisions: label each item decide, discuss or inform.

39
00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:54.258
A decision item is a question: do we extend the pilot to region North from week 12?

40
00:02:55.282 --> 00:02:59.578
Two days before, a decision note goes out: one page with question,

41
00:02:59.729 --> 00:03:02.004
options, recommendation, deadline.

42
00:03:02.735 --> 00:03:05.440
Information goes in writing, not the meeting.

43
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:11.363
The minutes, the written record: only decisions and actions, who does what by when,

44
00:03:11.502 --> 00:03:12.617
sent the same day.

45
00:03:13.628 --> 00:03:17.320
At the beverage plant: after the steering group, half a page,

46
00:03:17.494 --> 00:03:20.374
three decisions, four actions, sent at four.

47
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.745
Start every meeting with: which of last week's actions are done?

48
00:03:25.424 --> 00:03:28.315
Slipping actions say more than any status slide.

49
00:03:29.678 --> 00:03:31.907
Every meeting costs the client hours.

50
00:03:32.624 --> 00:03:37.675
Keep it short, start on time, end early, cancel when there is nothing to decide.

51
00:03:38.707 --> 00:03:40.298
The sponsor keeps cancelling?

52
00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.728
A signal, not bad luck.

53
00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:46.244
Ask whether he would rather decide another way.

54
00:03:47.248 --> 00:03:50.011
It goes wrong with twelve listeners at the weekly,

55
00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:52.310
and a slideshow at the steering group.

56
00:03:53.398 --> 00:03:53.990
How often?

57
00:03:54.693 --> 00:04:00.161
Experts disagree: full-time agile teams daily, part-time client teams twice a week.

58
00:04:00.838 --> 00:04:03.485
Stand-up running over, nobody naming a blocker?

59
00:04:04.223 --> 00:04:05.256
Change the format.

60
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.723
A review cascade: short meetings feeding each other,

61
00:04:09.839 --> 00:04:13.845
from team through department to management, with the same definitions.

62
00:04:14.874 --> 00:04:17.997
Escalation links them: a problem goes up one level,

63
00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.575
with a date by which the decision comes back.

64
00:04:21.626 --> 00:04:25.515
Look at a number as often as it really changes and someone can act:

65
00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:26.699
breakdowns daily,

66
00:04:26.897 --> 00:04:27.826
costs monthly.

67
00:04:28.606 --> 00:04:29.767
Else it is noise.

68
00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:36.609
A fixed agenda: actions, numbers against target, one or two problems, new actions.

69
00:04:37.308 --> 00:04:38.980
Same time, same place.

70
00:04:40.021 --> 00:04:41.821
And one visible action list.

71
00:04:42.597 --> 00:04:45.325
If nobody looks at it, the rhythm dies.

72
00:04:46.738 --> 00:04:51.532
Design for your exit from day one: the line chairs, you coach and step back.

73
00:04:52.298 --> 00:04:54.388
That handover is the real deliverable.

74
00:04:55.417 --> 00:05:00.003
A parts manufacturer: you have chaired the KPI review for eight weeks.

75
00:05:00.706 --> 00:05:02.076
In four weeks you leave.

76
00:05:03.128 --> 00:05:06.367
Week 1 the production manager chairs, you beside him.

77
00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:08.785
Week 2 you only attend.

78
00:05:09.565 --> 00:05:11.655
Weeks 3 and 4 you stay away.

79
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.406
The data refreshes without you.

80
00:05:15.108 --> 00:05:19.067
One page says who, when, which numbers, how problems go up.

81
00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:22.426
Plan checks after four and twelve weeks.

82
00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:27.461
Watch for meetings cancelled just this once, actions carried over,

83
00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.075
and a one-person board.

84
00:05:29.812 --> 00:05:33.887
Fifteen minutes a day with eight people costs 10 person-hours a week,

85
00:05:34.049 --> 00:05:35.315
so it must deliver more.

86
00:05:36.339 --> 00:05:40.043
Ask the owner: if I don't show up next week, will this meeting happen?
